<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Manchukuo</h1><p class="st-meta">1932 CE – 1945 CE · capital Hsinking (Changchun)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The puppet state that Japan created in north-east China in 1932, with the last Qing emperor Puyi as its figurehead ruler.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In September 1931 officers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a>, based in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kwantung-leased-territory">Kwantung Leased Territory</a>, staged an explosion on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-manchuria-railway">South Manchuria Railway</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Mukden</a> and used it as a pretext to occupy all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. In March 1932 they proclaimed the state of Manchukuo, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a> as chief executive and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchun">Hsinking</a> (Changchun) as capital; he was enthroned as emperor in 1934.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chinese ministers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-xiaoxu">Zheng Xiaoxu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-jinghui">Zhang Jinghui</a> held office, but Japanese vice-ministers and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> held real power. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> refused recognition, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> left the League in 1933. Manchukuo became a laboratory for state-planned heavy industry, mining and railways, and received Japanese and Korean settlers. Resistance was suppressed brutally, and Unit 731 conducted lethal human experiments near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> invaded in August 1945, and the state collapsed within days; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a> was captured. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> passed back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and its industries became a prize in the civil war.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/manchukuo.jpg" alt="Manchukuo" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Urmas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Manchukuo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1932 CE – 1945 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hsinking (Changchun)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Puyi, Zheng Xiaoxu (prime minister), Zhang Jinghui (prime minister)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese folk religion, Buddhism, State Shinto</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mandarin, Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Manchoukuo, Empire of Manchuria, Manchu State</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=manchukuo" aria-label="Manchukuo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7687 625 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Manchukuo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8007 881L8529 1145M8007 881L8221 1549"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8529 1145h0M8221 1549h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8007 881h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8007 881h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hsinking. <a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=manchukuo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
